// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 13): technology.
ADDART({id:"desalination",t:"Desalination",c:"Technology",i:"💧",w:"Desalination",r:["waterchem","droughts","mixtures","energyphys","saudicountry"],
p:[["Fresh water from the sea","Desalination removes salt from seawater to make drinking water."],["Reverse osmosis","Most new plants push water through very fine membranes."],["Thirsty regions","Desalination is vital in dry places such as the Middle East."]],
l:"Desalination is the process of removing salt and other minerals from seawater or salty groundwater to produce fresh water for drinking, farming and industry. Sailors have long boiled seawater and collected the steam, a method called distillation that was described by the Greek philosopher Aristotle. Large land-based plants spread in the 20th century, especially in the dry Middle East, using heat to evaporate seawater in many stages, a method known as multistage flash distillation. Today, most new plants use reverse osmosis, developed by Sidney Loeb and Srinivasa Sourirajan at the University of California, Los Angeles, around 1960, in which seawater is pumped at high pressure through membranes with pores so tiny that water molecules pass through but salt does not. A 2019 study counted nearly 16,000 desalination plants worldwide producing about 95 million cubic meters of fresh water a day, almost half of it in the Middle East and North Africa; Saudi Arabia is the largest producer. Desalination provides water independent of rain, but it uses a lot of energy and produces brine, very salty wastewater that can harm marine life if released carelessly. Engineers are working to power plants with solar energy and to reuse brine.",
s:[["Distillation","In thermal desalination, seawater is heated so that pure water evaporates, leaving salt behind; the steam is cooled and collected. Multistage flash plants reuse heat by boiling water at lower and lower pressures. These plants are often built next to power stations to use their waste heat."],["Reverse osmosis","Normally, water flows through a membrane from a less salty to a saltier solution, a process called osmosis. Reverse osmosis applies pressure to force water the opposite way, leaving salt behind. Improvements in membranes and energy-recovery devices have cut its energy use by more than half since the 1970s."],["Brine","For every liter of fresh water, a plant may produce more than a liter of brine. Discharged into the sea, it can sink and lower oxygen levels on the seabed. Plants spread brine through diffusers, and researchers are extracting useful minerals such as magnesium and lithium from it."],["Who depends on it?","Countries such as Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait and Qatar rely on desalination for most of their drinking water. Israel gets most of its household water from desalination, and cities such as Perth in Australia, Singapore and parts of California and Spain also use it."]],
k:[["Main method today","Reverse osmosis"],["Reverse osmosis developed","About 1960, UCLA"],["Plants worldwide","Nearly 16,000 (2019 study)"],["Output","About 95 million cubic meters a day"],["Largest producer","Saudi Arabia"]],
d:[["c. 320 BC","Aristotle describes making fresh water by evaporation"],["1869","Early land-based seawater distiller built in Aden"],["1960","Reverse osmosis membranes developed at UCLA"],["1965","First commercial reverse osmosis plant, Coalinga, California"],["2013","Sorek plant in Israel, one of the largest reverse osmosis plants, opens"]],
s2:[["Solar stills","Simple solar stills, in which sunlight evaporates water under a sloping glass or plastic cover, can provide small amounts of drinking water in deserts and lifeboats without any fuel."],["Water and energy","Desalinating one cubic meter of seawater by reverse osmosis takes about 3 to 4 kilowatt-hours of electricity. Linking plants to solar and wind power can reduce their carbon emissions."]],
sp:["Nature’s desalination","Seabirds such as albatrosses and penguins drink seawater and remove the salt with special glands above their eyes, sneezing or dripping out concentrated salty fluid."],
g:[["Desalination","Removing salt from water"],["Reverse osmosis","Forcing water through a membrane to remove salt"],["Brine","Very salty wastewater"],["Membrane","A thin sheet that lets some substances through"]],
f:["Most new plants use reverse osmosis.","Saudi Arabia produces the most desalinated water.","Desalination produces salty brine."],
fun:"Seabirds such as albatrosses have built-in desalination glands that let them drink seawater.",
tl:"Inaalis ng desalination ang asin sa tubig-dagat upang maging inuming tubig. Itinutulak ng karamihan sa mga bagong planta ang tubig sa napakapinong membrane, at mahalaga ito sa mga tuyong lugar gaya ng Middle East."});

ADDART({id:"webbrowsers",t:"Web Browsers",c:"Technology",i:"🌐",w:"Web browser",r:["worldwideweb","internet","searchengines","cybersecurity","opensource"],
p:[["Windows on the web","Web browsers are programs for viewing websites."],["The browser wars","Netscape and Internet Explorer battled in the 1990s."],["Chrome leads","Google Chrome is now the most used browser."]],
l:"A web browser is a software application for finding, displaying and interacting with pages on the World Wide Web. When a user types a web address or clicks a link, the browser requests the page from a server, then reads its HTML code, styles and scripts to show text, images, videos and interactive features. The first browser, called WorldWideWeb, was written by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN in 1990. Mosaic, released in 1993 by the National Center for Supercomputing Applications in the United States, displayed images alongside text and made the web popular. Its co-creator Marc Andreessen then helped launch Netscape Navigator in 1994, which quickly dominated. Microsoft released Internet Explorer in 1995 and bundled it free with Windows, winning the ‘browser wars’ and prompting a major US antitrust case. Open-source Firefox, released in 2004, and Apple’s Safari, launched in 2003, revived competition, and Google Chrome, launched in September 2008, became the world’s most popular browser, used for about two-thirds of web browsing. Many browsers, including Microsoft Edge, are now built on Chrome’s open-source Chromium code. Browsers also protect users by blocking dangerous sites and keeping websites separated from one another.",
s:[["How a browser works","A browser’s rendering engine turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into what appears on screen. Major engines include Blink (used by Chrome and Edge), WebKit (Safari) and Gecko (Firefox). Browsers also store cookies, cached files and history, which users can clear."],["The browser wars","In 1998, the US government sued Microsoft for using its Windows monopoly to push Internet Explorer. A court ruled against Microsoft in 2000; the company avoided being broken up but agreed to restrictions. Internet Explorer was officially retired in June 2022."],["Privacy","Browsers offer private modes, tracker blocking and settings to limit data collection. Some, such as Firefox, Safari and Brave, emphasize privacy features. Regulators in Europe have required choice screens so that users can pick their browser."],["Browsers today","Modern browsers run complex applications such as email, office software, games and video calls. Some newer browsers include artificial intelligence assistants that can summarize pages or carry out tasks online."]],
k:[["First browser","WorldWideWeb, 1990"],["First popular graphical browser","Mosaic, 1993"],["Internet Explorer","1995–2022"],["Most used browser","Google Chrome (launched 2008)"],["Open-source code base","Chromium"]],
d:[["1990","Tim Berners-Lee writes the first web browser"],["1993","Mosaic released"],["1994","Netscape Navigator released"],["1995","Internet Explorer released"],["2004","Firefox 1.0 released"],["2008","Google Chrome launched"],["2022","Internet Explorer retired"]],
s2:[["Tabs","Tabbed browsing, which lets users open several pages in one window, appeared in browsers in the late 1990s and early 2000s and was popularized by Opera and Firefox."],["Mozilla","After losing the browser wars, Netscape released its code as open source in 1998, founding the Mozilla project, which later produced Firefox."]],
sp:["The dinosaur game","When Chrome cannot connect to the internet, it shows a small dinosaur. Pressing the space bar starts a hidden running game in which the dinosaur jumps over cacti."],
g:[["Browser","A program for viewing websites"],["HTML","The code used to structure web pages"],["Cookie","A small file a website stores in a browser"],["Rendering engine","Software that draws web pages"]],
f:["The first web browser was written in 1990.","Mosaic made the web popular in 1993.","Chrome is the most used browser."],
fun:"When Google Chrome loses its internet connection, it offers a hidden game starring a pixel dinosaur.",
tl:"Ang web browser ay programang ginagamit sa pagtingin ng mga website. Naglaban ang Netscape at Internet Explorer noong dekada 1990, at Google Chrome na ngayon ang pinakaginagamit na browser."});

ADDART({id:"ipaddresses",t:"Internet Addresses and Domain Names",c:"Technology",i:"🔢",w:"Domain Name System",r:["internet","worldwideweb","webbrowsers","datacenters","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Every device has an address","IP addresses identify devices on the internet."],["Names for numbers","The Domain Name System turns names into IP addresses."],["Running out","The world ran out of old IPv4 addresses and is moving to IPv6."]],
l:"Every device connected to the internet uses an Internet Protocol (IP) address, a number that lets data find its way to the right place, much like a postal address. The original system, IPv4, introduced in the early 1980s, uses 32-bit addresses written as four numbers such as 192.0.2.1, allowing about 4.3 billion addresses. Because people remember names more easily than numbers, the Domain Name System (DNS), designed by Paul Mockapetris in 1983, translates names such as example.com into IP addresses; it works like a giant, distributed phone book spread across servers worldwide. The first .com domain, symbolics.com, was registered on 15 March 1985. Domain names are organized into top-level domains, including generic ones such as .com, .org and .net and country codes such as .ph, .uk and .jp. Since 1998, the nonprofit ICANN has coordinated the system. With billions of computers, phones and other devices online, the central supply of IPv4 addresses ran out in 2011. The newer IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses, allowing about 340 undecillion addresses, enough for every grain of sand on Earth many times over. In 2026, about half of users connecting to Google did so over IPv6.",
s:[["How DNS works","When you type a web address, your device asks a DNS resolver, usually run by your internet provider. The resolver asks root servers, then the servers for the top-level domain, and finally the domain’s own servers for the IP address. The answer is cached so later lookups are fast."],["Running out of IPv4","The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority handed out its last blocks of IPv4 addresses to regional registries in February 2011. Since then, networks have shared addresses using techniques such as network address translation, and IPv4 addresses are bought and sold."],["Country domains","Some small countries earn money from their domains: Tuvalu’s .tv is popular with video sites, and the British Indian Ocean Territory’s .io with tech companies. Anguilla’s .ai has become valuable because of interest in artificial intelligence."],["Security","Attackers sometimes register misspelled domain names to trick users, or attack DNS to redirect traffic. Security extensions called DNSSEC add digital signatures to DNS records."]],
k:[["IPv4 addresses","About 4.3 billion"],["IPv6 addresses","About 340 undecillion"],["DNS designed","1983, Paul Mockapetris"],["First .com domain","symbolics.com, 15 March 1985"],["Coordinator","ICANN (since 1998)"]],
d:[["1981","IPv4 standard published"],["1983","Domain Name System designed"],["1985","First .com domain registered"],["1998","ICANN founded"],["2011","Central IPv4 supply runs out"],["2012","World IPv6 Launch Day"]],
s2:[["Jon Postel","Computer scientist Jon Postel managed the assignment of internet addresses and names almost single-handedly for years, earning the nickname ‘God of the Internet’. He died in 1998, the year ICANN took over his role."],["Expensive names","Short, memorable domain names can sell for millions of dollars. Domains such as voice.com and cars.com have changed hands for more than 10 million dollars each."]],
sp:["Your address","Most home devices share one public IP address, given by the internet provider, while the router assigns private addresses inside the home. That is why many devices can use the internet through one connection."],
g:[["IP address","A number identifying a device on a network"],["DNS","The system that turns names into IP addresses"],["Top-level domain","The last part of a domain name, such as .com"],["IPv6","The newer version of internet addresses"]],
f:["DNS turns names into numbers.","The first .com domain was registered in 1985.","IPv6 has about 340 undecillion addresses."],
fun:"IPv6 has enough addresses to give trillions of addresses to every person on Earth.",
tl:"May IP address ang bawat device sa internet. Isinasalin ng Domain Name System ang mga pangalan sa IP address, at lumilipat na ang mundo sa IPv6 matapos maubos ang lumang IPv4."});

ADDART({id:"mobilenetworks",t:"Mobile Phone Networks: 1G to 5G",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Cellular network",r:["phone","smartphone","radio","textmessaging","wifi"],
p:[["Calls through cells","Mobile networks divide land into cells, each with an antenna."],["A new generation each decade","From 1G to 5G, networks became faster and digital."],["Billions on 5G","More than 3 billion people use 5G."]],
l:"Mobile phone networks, also called cellular networks, let phones communicate by radio while moving. The land is divided into areas called cells, each served by a base station with antennas, often on towers or rooftops; as a user travels, calls and data are handed from one cell to the next. The idea was proposed at Bell Labs in 1947, but it needed computers and electronics to become practical. The first generation (1G) of analog networks opened in Tokyo in 1979 and in the United States in 1983. Second-generation (2G) digital networks, led by the European GSM standard, began in Finland in 1991 and introduced text messages. Third-generation (3G) networks, starting in Japan in 2001, brought mobile internet, and fourth-generation (4G LTE) networks, launched in Stockholm and Oslo in 2009, made video streaming and apps such as ride-hailing practical. Fifth-generation (5G) networks, launched in South Korea and the United States in April 2019, offer much higher speeds and lower delays, and by 2026 more than 3 billion subscriptions used 5G. The next generation, 6G, is expected around 2030. Mobile networks have brought phones to billions of people who never had landlines.",
s:[["How cells work","Each cell uses a set of radio frequencies, and cells farther apart can reuse the same frequencies, letting a network serve millions of users. Cells are smaller in crowded cities and larger in the countryside. When a phone moves, the network performs a ‘handoff’ to the next cell without dropping the call."],["The generations","Each generation brought a big change: 1G carried analog voice, 2G digital voice and texts, 3G mobile internet, 4G fast broadband and 5G very high speeds, quick response and connections for huge numbers of devices such as sensors and machines."],["Spectrum","Governments auction radio frequencies, called spectrum, to mobile companies, sometimes for billions of dollars. Higher frequencies carry more data but travel shorter distances, so high-speed 5G needs many small cells."],["Health concerns","Some people worry that radio waves from phones and masts cause illness, and false claims linked 5G to COVID-19 in 2020. Health agencies, including the World Health Organization, say there is no established evidence that mobile network signals within safety limits harm health."]],
k:[["Cell idea","Bell Labs, 1947"],["1G launch","Tokyo, 1979"],["2G (GSM) launch","Finland, 1991"],["4G launch","Stockholm and Oslo, 2009"],["5G launch","April 2019"]],
d:[["1947","Cellular concept proposed at Bell Labs"],["1979","First 1G network opens in Tokyo"],["1991","First GSM call in Finland"],["2001","First 3G network in Japan"],["2009","First 4G LTE networks"],["2019","5G networks launch"],["2026","5G subscriptions pass 3 billion"]],
s2:[["Mobile money","In Kenya, the mobile money service M-Pesa, launched in 2007, lets people send and receive money by text message. Such services have brought banking to millions without bank accounts."],["Disaster networks","After disasters, companies deploy portable cell towers on trucks or drones and satellite connections to restore phone service quickly."]],
sp:["The first GSM call","On 1 July 1991, Finnish Prime Minister Harri Holkeri made the first call on a GSM network, speaking to the deputy mayor of Tampere."],
g:[["Cell","An area served by one base station"],["Base station","Antennas and equipment serving a cell"],["Handoff","Passing a call from one cell to another"],["Spectrum","The range of radio frequencies"]],
f:["The first 1G network opened in Tokyo in 1979.","GSM began in Finland in 1991.","More than 3 billion subscriptions use 5G."],
fun:"Phones constantly ‘talk’ to nearby base stations even when not in use, so the network always knows which cell they are in.",
tl:"Hinahati ng mobile network ang lupa sa mga cell na may kani-kaniyang antenna. Mula 1G hanggang 5G, bumilis at naging digital ang mga network, at mahigit 3 bilyong subscription na ang gumagamit ng 5G."});

ADDART({id:"combustionengines",t:"Internal Combustion Engines",c:"Technology",i:"🔥",w:"Internal combustion engine",r:["cars","thermo","fossilfuels","ev","motorcycles"],
p:[["Power from burning fuel","Internal combustion engines burn fuel inside cylinders."],["Four strokes","Intake, compression, power and exhaust turn the crankshaft."],["Cars, ships and planes","They powered the 20th century’s transport revolution."]],
l:"An internal combustion engine produces power by burning fuel inside the engine itself, unlike a steam engine, which burns fuel outside to boil water. In most engines, a mixture of fuel and air explodes inside cylinders, pushing pistons that turn a crankshaft. Belgian-French engineer Étienne Lenoir built the first commercially successful internal combustion engine in 1860. In 1876, German engineer Nikolaus Otto built a four-stroke engine, the basis of most car engines today. Karl Benz used a gasoline engine in his three-wheeled car of 1885–1886, and Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built light, fast-running engines. Rudolf Diesel applied for a patent on his engine in 1892; it ignites fuel by compressing air until it becomes hot, making it efficient for trucks, ships, trains and generators. Internal combustion engines powered cars, motorcycles, tractors, early aircraft and ships, transforming transport and farming. However, they burn fossil fuels, releasing carbon dioxide, which drives climate change, and pollutants that harm health. Electric vehicles are increasingly replacing them, and several governments have set dates to phase out sales of new petrol and diesel cars, though some have softened these plans.",
s:[["The four-stroke cycle","In the intake stroke, the piston moves down and draws in air and fuel. In compression, it moves up and squeezes the mixture. A spark plug ignites it, forcing the piston down in the power stroke. Finally, the exhaust stroke pushes out burned gases. Many cylinders work in turn for smooth power."],["Diesel engines","Diesel engines have no spark plugs. They compress air so strongly that it becomes hot enough to ignite fuel sprayed in. They are more efficient than gasoline engines and produce high turning force, but older diesels emit soot and nitrogen oxides."],["Efficiency","Most car engines turn only about 25 to 40 percent of the energy in fuel into motion; the rest is lost as heat. Large ship diesel engines can exceed 50 percent efficiency."],["Cleaner engines","Catalytic converters, introduced in the 1970s, convert harmful gases into less harmful ones. Hybrid cars combine engines with electric motors and batteries. Some engines can run on biofuels or hydrogen."]],
k:[["First commercial engine","Étienne Lenoir, 1860"],["Four-stroke engine","Nikolaus Otto, 1876"],["Diesel engine patent","Rudolf Diesel, 1892"],["Typical car efficiency","About 25–40%"],["Main fuels","Gasoline and diesel"]],
d:[["1860","Lenoir’s gas engine"],["1876","Otto’s four-stroke engine"],["1886","Benz patents his motor car"],["1892","Diesel applies for a patent on his engine"],["1903","Wright brothers fly with a gasoline engine"],["1975","Catalytic converters required on new US cars"]],
s2:[["Rudolf Diesel","Rudolf Diesel disappeared from a ship crossing the English Channel in 1913. His body was found days later, and the cause of his death remains a mystery."],["Two-stroke engines","Two-stroke engines complete the cycle in two piston movements. They are light and simple, used in chainsaws, some motorcycles and outboard boat motors, but they tend to pollute more."]],
sp:["Horsepower","Engine power is often measured in horsepower, a unit invented by James Watt in the 18th century to compare steam engines with the work of horses."],
g:[["Piston","A part that moves up and down in a cylinder"],["Crankshaft","A shaft that turns up-and-down motion into rotation"],["Spark plug","A device that ignites fuel with a spark"],["Catalytic converter","A device that cleans exhaust gases"]],
f:["Otto built the four-stroke engine in 1876.","Diesel engines ignite fuel by compression.","Most car engines waste much of their energy as heat."],
fun:"Rudolf Diesel showed an engine running on peanut oil at the 1900 Paris World’s Fair.",
tl:"Gumagawa ng lakas ang internal combustion engine sa pagsunog ng gasolina sa loob ng mga silindro. Iniikot ng apat na stroke ang crankshaft, at pinatakbo ng mga makinang ito ang rebolusyon sa transportasyon noong ika-20 siglo."});

ADDART({id:"gears",t:"Gears",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Gear",r:["machines","clocks","bicycle","machinetools","robot"],
p:[["Wheels with teeth","Gears are toothed wheels that pass motion between shafts."],["Speed or strength","Gears can increase speed or increase turning force."],["An ancient computer","The Antikythera mechanism used gears over 2,000 years ago."]],
l:"Gears are wheels with teeth that mesh with other gears to transmit rotation and force from one part of a machine to another. When a small gear drives a larger one, the larger gear turns more slowly but with more turning force, called torque; when a large gear drives a small one, the small gear spins faster. This relationship, the gear ratio, lets machines trade speed for strength. Gears also change the direction of motion. Ancient Greek and Chinese engineers used gears; the Antikythera mechanism, found in a shipwreck off Greece and made around the 2nd or 1st century BC, used at least 30 bronze gears to predict the positions of the Sun, Moon and eclipses, and is often called the first analog computer. Medieval mechanical clocks depended on gears, and gears were central to mills, the Industrial Revolution and cars. There are many types: spur gears with straight teeth, bevel gears that turn corners, worm gears that give huge reductions in speed, rack-and-pinion gears that turn rotation into straight-line motion and planetary gears in car transmissions. Gears are found in bicycles, watches, robots, wind turbines and kitchen mixers.",
s:[["Gear ratios","If a gear with 10 teeth drives a gear with 40 teeth, the larger gear turns once for every four turns of the smaller gear, a ratio of 4:1, and delivers about four times the torque. Bicycles use different-sized sprockets connected by a chain so riders can climb hills or go fast."],["Types of gears","Helical gears have angled teeth that run more quietly. Worm gears can lift heavy loads and often cannot be turned backward, which makes them useful in lifts and tuning pegs on guitars. Planetary gear sets, with small gears circling a central one, are compact and strong."],["Making gears","Gears must be shaped precisely so their teeth roll smoothly against each other. The involute tooth shape, described by mathematician Leonhard Euler in the 18th century, is used in most modern gears."],["Gears in nature","In 2013, scientists discovered that young planthoppers, small jumping insects, have interlocking gear-like teeth on their legs that synchronize their jumps, the first gears found in a living creature."]],
k:[["Purpose","Transmit rotation and force"],["Ancient example","Antikythera mechanism (2nd–1st century BC)"],["Key concept","Gear ratio"],["Common types","Spur, bevel, worm, rack and pinion, planetary"],["Gears in nature","Planthopper insects"]],
d:[["c. 150–100 BC","Antikythera mechanism made"],["c. 1300","Gear-driven mechanical clocks appear in Europe"],["1901","Antikythera mechanism found by sponge divers"],["1903","Early multi-speed bicycle hubs"],["2013","Natural gears discovered in planthoppers"]],
s2:[["The Antikythera mechanism","Sponge divers found the mechanism in a Roman-era shipwreck in 1901. X-ray and computer scans in the 2000s revealed hidden inscriptions and gears that tracked the Moon’s phases and the four-year cycle of the ancient Olympic Games."],["Clockwork","Mechanical clocks and watches use gear trains to divide the energy of a spring or weight into precise movements of the hands. Some watches contain hundreds of tiny parts."]],
sp:["Gear-driven toys","Wind-up toys use a spring and a series of gears. The gears slow the spring’s energy so the toy moves for longer."],
g:[["Gear","A toothed wheel"],["Torque","Turning force"],["Gear ratio","The relationship between the turning speeds of gears"],["Transmission","A gear system that changes speed in a vehicle"]],
f:["Gears trade speed for strength.","The Antikythera mechanism used gears 2,000 years ago.","Planthopper insects have natural gears."],
fun:"Tiny insects called planthoppers have gears on their legs that keep their legs moving together when they jump.",
tl:"Ang gear ay gulong na may ngipin na naglilipat ng galaw sa pagitan ng mga baras. Kayang pabilisin o palakasin ng gear ang pag-ikot, at gumamit ng mga gear ang Antikythera mechanism mahigit 2,000 taon na ang nakalipas."});

ADDART({id:"hydraulics",t:"Hydraulics and Pneumatics",c:"Technology",i:"🛠️",w:"Hydraulics",r:["fluids","constructionmachines","machines","flight","cars"],
p:[["Power from fluids","Hydraulics uses liquids under pressure to move heavy loads."],["Pascal’s principle","Pressure in a liquid spreads equally in all directions."],["Air power","Pneumatics uses compressed air instead of liquid."]],
l:"Hydraulics is the use of liquids under pressure to transmit force and power machines, while pneumatics does the same with compressed air or gas. Both rely on the fact that pressure applied to a fluid in a closed system spreads equally in all directions, a rule described by French scientist Blaise Pascal in the 17th century. Because of this, a small force pushing on a small piston can create a much larger force on a large piston, letting people lift cars or crush metal with little effort. Englishman Joseph Bramah patented the hydraulic press in 1795. In the 19th century, cities such as London had networks of pipes carrying pressurized water to power cranes, lifts and bridges; London’s Tower Bridge originally raised its roadways with hydraulic power. Today, hydraulics moves the arms of excavators and cranes, operates car brakes, controls aircraft wings and landing gear and powers factory presses. Pneumatics drives air brakes on trucks and trains, invented by George Westinghouse in 1869, as well as jackhammers, dental drills, nail guns and the pneumatic tubes that once carried messages through buildings. Robots and amusement rides often use both.",
s:[["Pascal’s principle","If a piston with an area of 1 square centimeter pushes on oil connected to a piston with an area of 100 square centimeters, the larger piston pushes with 100 times the force, though it moves only a hundredth as far. This is how a car jack lets one person lift a car."],["Hydraulic fluids","Hydraulic systems usually use oil because it does not compress, lubricates parts and resists rust. Leaks can be dangerous: fluid under high pressure can pierce skin, and some fluids are flammable."],["Pneumatics","Air is compressible, which makes pneumatic systems springy and less precise but cleaner and safer than hydraulics. Compressors store air in tanks. Pneumatic tools are popular in factories because air is cheap and leaks are harmless."],["Car brakes","Pressing a car’s brake pedal pushes fluid through tubes to the brakes at each wheel, multiplying the driver’s force to squeeze brake pads against discs and stop the car."]],
k:[["Key principle","Pascal’s principle"],["Hydraulic press","Joseph Bramah, 1795"],["Air brake","George Westinghouse, 1869"],["Hydraulic fluid","Usually oil"],["Uses","Excavators, brakes, aircraft, presses"]],
d:[["1653","Pascal describes the principle of pressure in fluids"],["1795","Bramah patents the hydraulic press"],["1869","Westinghouse invents the railway air brake"],["1883","London Hydraulic Power Company founded"],["1894","Tower Bridge opens, raised by hydraulic power"]],
s2:[["Pneumatic tube mail","From the 1850s, cities such as London, Paris, Berlin and New York sent letters through underground pneumatic tubes. Prague’s system ran until 2002, and many hospitals still send samples by pneumatic tube."],["Hydraulics in nature","Spiders extend their legs using fluid pressure rather than muscles, a form of hydraulics. That is why dead spiders curl up their legs."]],
sp:["Water-powered London","At its peak, London’s hydraulic power network had about 290 kilometers of pipes carrying high-pressure water to power lifts, cranes and even theater stages."],
g:[["Hydraulics","Using pressurized liquid to transmit power"],["Pneumatics","Using compressed air to transmit power"],["Piston","A sliding part pushed by fluid pressure"],["Compressor","A machine that squeezes air into a tank"]],
f:["Hydraulics multiplies force using liquids.","Air brakes were invented in 1869.","Spiders use hydraulics to extend their legs."],
fun:"Spiders stretch their legs using fluid pressure, which is why their legs curl up when they die.",
tl:"Ginagamit ng hydraulics ang likidong may presyon upang buhatin ang mabibigat na bagay. Kumakalat nang pantay ang presyon sa likido, at naka-compress na hangin naman ang ginagamit ng pneumatics."});

ADDART({id:"vacuumtubes",t:"Vacuum Tubes",c:"Technology",i:"💡",w:"Vacuum tube",r:["radio","tv","comp","chip","electron"],
p:[["The first electronics","Vacuum tubes controlled electric currents before transistors."],["Radio and television","They made radio, TV and early computers possible."],["Still glowing","Guitar amplifiers and microwave ovens still use tubes."]],
l:"A vacuum tube, also called a valve, is a sealed glass or metal container with most of the air removed, inside which electrodes control the flow of electrons. When a filament inside is heated, it gives off electrons that flow to a positive plate. In 1904, English engineer John Ambrose Fleming invented the diode tube, which lets current flow in only one direction and could detect radio signals. In 1906, American inventor Lee de Forest added a third electrode, a grid, creating the triode, or Audion, which could amplify weak signals. Vacuum tubes launched the electronics age, making possible radio broadcasting, long-distance telephone calls, sound films, television, radar and the first electronic computers. ENIAC, completed in 1945, used about 17,500 tubes, filled a large room and used as much power as a small town. Tubes were large, fragile, hot and burned out often, so the invention of the transistor at Bell Labs in 1947 gradually replaced them. However, some tubes remain in use: cavity magnetrons in microwave ovens, high-power radio and radar transmitters, X-ray machines and guitar amplifiers prized for their warm sound.",
s:[["How a tube works","In a triode, a small voltage on the grid controls a much larger flow of electrons from the hot cathode to the plate, so a weak signal becomes a strong one. This ability to amplify was essential for radio receivers, loudspeakers and telephone repeaters."],["The Edison effect","In 1883, Thomas Edison noticed that current could flow through the vacuum inside his light bulbs from the hot filament to a separate wire. He patented the effect but did not find a use for it; Fleming later used it in his diode."],["Tube computers","Early computers such as Britain’s Colossus (1943–1944) and ENIAC used thousands of tubes as switches. Engineers had to replace failed tubes frequently, and insects attracted by warmth sometimes caused faults."],["Cathode-ray tubes","Television sets and computer monitors used a special vacuum tube called a cathode-ray tube, which fired electrons at a glowing screen, until flat screens replaced them in the 2000s."]],
k:[["Diode tube","John Ambrose Fleming, 1904"],["Triode (Audion)","Lee de Forest, 1906"],["ENIAC tubes","About 17,500"],["Replaced by","Transistors (from 1947)"],["Still used in","Microwave ovens, guitar amplifiers, transmitters"]],
d:[["1883","Edison observes the Edison effect"],["1904","Fleming invents the diode tube"],["1906","De Forest invents the triode"],["1920","Regular radio broadcasting begins"],["1945","ENIAC completed"],["1947","Transistor invented"]],
s2:[["Radio’s golden age","Vacuum tube radios brought news, music and entertainment into homes from the 1920s. Families gathered around large wooden radio sets that took time to ‘warm up’ as their tubes heated."],["Warm sound","Many musicians prefer tube amplifiers for electric guitars because when pushed hard they distort in a smooth way that many find pleasant, which is why tubes are still made today."]],
sp:["Valves","In British English, vacuum tubes are called ‘valves’ because, like a water valve, they control a flow, in this case of electrons."],
g:[["Vacuum tube","A sealed tube controlling electron flow"],["Diode","A device allowing current in one direction"],["Triode","A tube with three electrodes that can amplify"],["Amplify","To make a signal stronger"]],
f:["The triode was invented in 1906.","ENIAC used about 17,500 vacuum tubes.","Microwave ovens still use a type of tube."],
fun:"The magnetron that heats food in every microwave oven is a type of vacuum tube.",
tl:"Kinokontrol ng vacuum tube ang kuryente bago pa nagkaroon ng transistor. Pinasimulan nito ang radyo, TV at mga unang computer, at ginagamit pa rin ito sa mga guitar amplifier at microwave oven."});

ADDART({id:"punchcards",t:"Punched Cards",c:"Technology",i:"🗂️",w:"Punched card",r:["comp","weaving","stats","coding","indrev"],
p:[["Data in holes","Punched cards stored information as patterns of holes."],["From looms to computers","They controlled weaving looms before computers."],["The 1890 census","Punched cards sped up counting the US census."]],
l:"Punched cards are stiff paper cards that store information as patterns of holes in specific positions, read by machines that detect where holes are. They were the main way of entering programs and data into computers for much of the 20th century. In 1804, French weaver Joseph Marie Jacquard built a loom controlled by chains of punched cards that automatically wove complex patterns, an early example of a programmable machine. English mathematician Charles Babbage planned to use punched cards to program his Analytical Engine in the 1830s. American inventor Herman Hollerith developed electric tabulating machines that read punched cards for the 1890 US census, finishing the count much faster than the hand-tallied 1880 census, which had taken about eight years. Hollerith’s company later became part of IBM, whose 80-column card, introduced in 1928, became a standard. Through the 1970s, programmers typed code onto stacks of cards using keypunch machines, one line per card, and dropping a stack could be a disaster. Cards were replaced by keyboards, terminals and magnetic storage, but their 80-character line width influenced computer screens for decades.",
s:[["Jacquard’s loom","Each card in a Jacquard loom controlled which threads were lifted for one row of weaving. Long chains of cards could weave portraits and detailed patterns. The loom inspired Babbage and showed that machines could follow stored instructions."],["Hollerith and the census","Hollerith’s machines counted holes using metal pins that completed electric circuits when they passed through holes. They were used in many countries’ censuses and in business accounting, railways and insurance."],["Programming with cards","Programmers wrote programs on paper, then punched them onto cards and handed the deck to computer operators. Results might come back hours later. A single error meant re-punching cards and waiting again."],["Hanging chads","Punched-card voting machines were used in US elections until the 2000s. In the close 2000 presidential election in Florida, partly punched holes, called ‘hanging chads’, led to disputes and a push for new voting technology."]],
k:[["Jacquard loom","1804"],["Hollerith tabulator","Used for the 1890 US census"],["IBM 80-column card","1928"],["Peak use","1950s–1970s"],["Replaced by","Terminals and magnetic storage"]],
d:[["1804","Jacquard’s punched-card loom"],["1837","Babbage designs the Analytical Engine"],["1890","Hollerith machines count the US census"],["1924","Hollerith’s company renamed IBM"],["1928","IBM introduces the 80-column card"],["2000","‘Hanging chads’ dispute in Florida"]],
s2:[["Do not fold","Punched cards often carried the warning ‘Do not fold, spindle or mutilate’, because bent or torn cards jammed machines. The phrase became a slogan in the 1960s for people who felt treated like numbers."],["Ada Lovelace","Ada Lovelace, writing about Babbage’s Analytical Engine in 1843, noted that it would weave algebraic patterns just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves."]],
sp:["80 columns","Early computer terminals displayed 80 characters per line to match punched cards, and many programmers still keep lines of code under 80 characters."],
g:[["Punched card","A card storing data as holes"],["Tabulator","A machine that counts data"],["Keypunch","A machine for punching cards"],["Census","An official count of a population"]],
f:["Jacquard’s loom used punched cards in 1804.","Punched cards counted the 1890 US census.","Programmers once wrote code on stacks of cards."],
fun:"The 80-character line width of old punched cards still influences how wide many programmers make their lines of code.",
tl:"Nag-iimbak ng impormasyon ang punched card sa anyo ng mga butas. Kinontrol nito ang mga habihan bago pa ang computer, at pinabilis nito ang pagbilang sa census ng US noong 1890."});

ADDART({id:"personalcomputers",t:"The Personal Computer",c:"Technology",i:"🖥️",w:"Personal computer",r:["comp","chip","operatingsystems","laptops","videogames"],
p:[["A computer for everyone","Personal computers brought computing into homes and offices."],["The 1970s revolution","The Altair, Apple II and others started the PC era."],["The IBM PC","IBM’s 1981 PC set a standard still followed today."]],
l:"A personal computer (PC) is a computer designed to be used by one person at a time, small and affordable enough for homes, schools and offices. Before the 1970s, computers were huge, expensive machines run by experts for governments, universities and large companies. The invention of the microprocessor, a whole computer processor on one chip, by Intel in 1971 made personal computers possible. The Altair 8800, a build-it-yourself kit featured on the cover of Popular Electronics magazine in January 1975, inspired hobbyists, including Bill Gates and Paul Allen, who founded Microsoft, and Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak, who founded Apple in 1976. In 1977, the Apple II, Commodore PET and Tandy TRS-80 became the first popular ready-made personal computers. IBM released its IBM PC on 12 August 1981, and because other companies could copy its design, ‘IBM-compatible’ PCs running Microsoft software came to dominate. Apple’s Macintosh, launched in January 1984, popularized the mouse and graphical user interface with windows and icons. Personal computers transformed work, education and entertainment, and about 250 million are still sold each year, most of them laptops.",
s:[["Xerox PARC","Researchers at Xerox’s Palo Alto Research Center developed the Alto in 1973, with a mouse, windows, icons and networking. Xerox did not sell it widely, but Steve Jobs saw it in 1979, and its ideas shaped the Macintosh and Microsoft Windows."],["Home computers","In the early 1980s, cheaper home computers such as the Commodore 64, ZX Spectrum and BBC Micro introduced millions of children to programming and games. The Commodore 64 is often cited as the best-selling single computer model."],["Windows","Microsoft Windows, first released in 1985, became popular with Windows 3.0 in 1990 and Windows 95, launched with huge publicity. Windows has run on most personal computers since the 1990s."],["Machine of the Year","Time magazine named the personal computer its ‘Machine of the Year’ for 1982 instead of choosing a Person of the Year."]],
k:[["Microprocessor","Intel 4004, 1971"],["Early kit","Altair 8800, 1975"],["Popular ready-made PCs","Apple II, Commodore PET, TRS-80 (1977)"],["IBM PC released","12 August 1981"],["Macintosh launched","January 1984"]],
d:[["1971","Intel 4004 microprocessor"],["1973","Xerox Alto developed"],["1975","Altair 8800; Microsoft founded"],["1976","Apple founded"],["1977","Apple II, Commodore PET and TRS-80 released"],["1981","IBM PC released"],["1984","Apple Macintosh launched"],["1995","Windows 95 released"]],
s2:[["The 1984 advertisement","Apple introduced the Macintosh with a famous television advertisement during the 1984 Super Bowl, directed by Ridley Scott, showing a woman smashing a screen showing a Big Brother figure."],["Garage beginnings","Apple, Hewlett-Packard and other tech companies are often said to have started in garages, symbols of Silicon Valley’s do-it-yourself spirit."]],
sp:["The first mouse","Douglas Engelbart and Bill English built the first computer mouse in the 1960s from a wooden block with two wheels. It was shown publicly in 1968."],
g:[["PC","Personal computer"],["Microprocessor","A computer processor on a single chip"],["Graphical user interface","Controlling a computer with windows and icons"],["Compatible","Able to run the same software"]],
f:["The Altair 8800 appeared in 1975.","The IBM PC was released in 1981.","The Macintosh popularized the mouse."],
fun:"Time magazine named the personal computer ‘Machine of the Year’ for 1982.",
tl:"Dinala ng personal computer ang computing sa mga tahanan at opisina. Sinimulan ng Altair, Apple II at iba pa ang panahon ng PC noong dekada 1970, at nagtakda ng pamantayan ang IBM PC noong 1981."});

ADDART({id:"typography",t:"Fonts and Typography",c:"Technology",i:"🔤",w:"Typography",r:["print","writing","calligraphy","personalcomputers","unicode"],
p:[["The art of letters","Typography is the design and arrangement of type."],["Serif or sans serif","Fonts with small strokes are serif; plain ones are sans serif."],["Fonts for everyone","Computers gave everyone a choice of fonts."]],
l:"Typography is the art and technique of arranging letters and text so that it is readable and attractive. A typeface is a design for a set of letters, such as Times New Roman or Helvetica, while a font originally meant a complete set of metal type in one size and style; today the words are often used interchangeably. Movable type was first invented in China by Bi Sheng in the 1040s, and Korean printers cast metal type in the 13th century. Johannes Gutenberg’s printing press, around 1450, spread movable metal type in Europe. Over the centuries, type designers such as Claude Garamond, John Baskerville and Giambattista Bodoni created influential typefaces. Typefaces are broadly divided into serif faces, with small finishing strokes at the ends of letters, and sans-serif faces, without them. Helvetica, designed in Switzerland in 1957, became one of the most widely used typefaces, and Times New Roman was created for The Times newspaper of London in 1932. The Apple Macintosh, launched in 1984, offered several fonts and proportional spacing, and today digital fonts let anyone choose from thousands of typefaces for documents, websites and signs.",
s:[["Serif and sans serif","Serif typefaces, such as Times New Roman and Garamond, are traditional in books and newspapers. Sans-serif typefaces, such as Helvetica, Arial and Verdana, look clean and modern and are common on screens and signs. Monospaced fonts, where every letter is the same width, are used for computer code."],["Measuring type","Type size is measured in points; in digital typography, a point is 1/72 of an inch. Designers also adjust kerning, the space between pairs of letters, and leading, the space between lines, a word that comes from strips of lead once placed between lines of metal type."],["Readability","Some typefaces are designed for special purposes: road signs use clear typefaces readable at speed, and fonts for people with dyslexia use distinct letter shapes. Large x-heights, the height of lowercase letters, can improve legibility."],["Steve Jobs and calligraphy","Steve Jobs said a calligraphy class he took after dropping out of college inspired the Macintosh’s range of beautiful fonts, which influenced all later personal computers."]],
k:[["First movable type","Bi Sheng, China, 1040s"],["Gutenberg press","About 1450"],["Helvetica designed","1957, Switzerland"],["Times New Roman","1932, for The Times"],["Unit of type size","Point (1/72 inch)"]],
d:[["1040s","Bi Sheng invents movable type"],["1377","Jikji, oldest surviving book printed with metal type, made in Korea"],["c. 1450","Gutenberg’s printing press"],["1932","Times New Roman introduced"],["1957","Helvetica designed"],["1984","Macintosh brings fonts to personal computers"]],
s2:[["Comic Sans","Comic Sans, designed by Vincent Connare for Microsoft in 1994 for speech bubbles in software, became hugely popular and also widely mocked when used for serious documents. Some teachers and dyslexia groups find it easy to read."],["Uppercase and lowercase","The terms ‘uppercase’ and ‘lowercase’ come from the cases, or trays, in which printers stored metal type: capital letters were kept in the upper case and small letters in the lower one."]],
sp:["Mind your p’s and q’s","One theory for the phrase ‘mind your p’s and q’s’ is that printers setting metal type, which is mirror-reversed, had to be careful not to confuse lowercase p and q."],
g:[["Typeface","A design for a set of letters"],["Serif","A small stroke at the end of a letter"],["Kerning","Adjusting space between letters"],["Point","A unit for measuring type size"]],
f:["Movable type was invented in China.","Helvetica was designed in 1957.","‘Uppercase’ comes from printers’ type cases."],
fun:"The words ‘uppercase’ and ‘lowercase’ come from the wooden cases in which printers stored capital and small letters.",
tl:"Ang typography ay ang disenyo at pag-aayos ng mga titik. Serif ang mga font na may maliliit na guhit sa dulo at sans serif ang mga payak, at binigyan ng computer ang lahat ng pagpipilian ng font."});

ADDART({id:"screens",t:"Display Screens",c:"Technology",i:"📺",w:"Display device",r:["tv","touchscreens","leds","light","smartphone"],
p:[["Pictures made of pixels","Screens build images from millions of tiny dots called pixels."],["From tubes to flat panels","Bulky cathode-ray tubes gave way to flat LCD and OLED screens."],["Everywhere","Screens are in phones, cars, watches and billboards."]],
l:"Display screens show images by controlling millions of tiny picture elements, called pixels, each of which can glow in different colors. Most color screens make every pixel from red, green and blue subpixels, which combine to produce millions of colors. For most of the 20th century, televisions and computer monitors used cathode-ray tubes (CRTs), invented by German physicist Ferdinand Braun in 1897, which fired beams of electrons at a phosphor-coated screen. CRTs were heavy and deep. Liquid crystals, substances that flow like liquids but have ordered molecules like crystals, were discovered in 1888, and in 1968 engineers at RCA led by George Heilmeier demonstrated the first liquid-crystal display (LCD). LCDs use liquid crystals to block or pass light from a backlight and became common in calculators and watches in the 1970s, laptops in the 1990s and televisions in the 2000s. Organic light-emitting diode (OLED) displays, developed at Kodak in 1987, have pixels that produce their own light, giving deep blacks and allowing thin, flexible and folding screens. Screens now appear in phones, cars, watches, refrigerators and giant stadium displays, and researchers are developing microLED and transparent screens.",
s:[["Resolution","Resolution is the number of pixels a screen shows. A 4K television has about 3,840 by 2,160 pixels, more than 8 million in total. On phones, pixels are so small that individual dots are invisible at normal viewing distance."],["LCD","An LCD sandwiches liquid crystals between polarizing filters. Applying voltage twists or untwists the crystals, controlling how much backlight passes through each subpixel. Modern LCDs use LED backlights and are cheap and bright."],["OLED","In OLED screens, each subpixel contains organic compounds that glow when electricity flows through them. Because black pixels are simply switched off, OLEDs show true blacks and save power. They are used in many phones, watches and high-end TVs."],["Refresh rate","Screens redraw images many times per second. Most refresh 60 times a second, while gaming monitors and some phones refresh 120 times or more for smoother motion."]],
k:[["Cathode-ray tube","Ferdinand Braun, 1897"],["Liquid crystals discovered","1888"],["First LCD","RCA, 1968"],["OLED developed","Kodak, 1987"],["Color system","Red, green and blue subpixels"]],
d:[["1888","Friedrich Reinitzer discovers liquid crystals"],["1897","Braun invents the cathode-ray tube"],["1968","First liquid-crystal display demonstrated"],["1987","Kodak scientists develop the OLED"],["2000s","Flat-panel TVs replace CRT sets"],["2019","Folding phone screens go on sale"]],
s2:[["Plasma screens","Plasma screens, in which tiny cells of gas glowed like miniature fluorescent lamps, were popular for large televisions in the 2000s but were overtaken by cheaper and more efficient LCDs."],["E-paper","Electronic paper displays, used in e-readers, reflect light like ink on paper and use power only when the image changes, so they can show a page for weeks."]],
sp:["Burn-in","Old CRT and plasma screens could suffer ‘burn-in’, where a fixed image left a ghostly mark. Screen savers were invented partly to prevent this."],
g:[["Pixel","A tiny dot of a digital image"],["LCD","Liquid-crystal display"],["OLED","Organic light-emitting diode display"],["Resolution","The number of pixels on a screen"]],
f:["Screens make colors from red, green and blue.","The first LCD was shown in 1968.","OLED pixels make their own light."],
fun:"Screen savers were originally invented to stop images from burning permanently into old monitors.",
tl:"Bumubuo ng larawan ang mga screen mula sa milyun-milyong maliliit na tuldok na tinatawag na pixel. Napalitan ng mga patag na LCD at OLED ang malalaking cathode-ray tube, at nasa telepono, kotse at relo na ang mga screen."});

ADDART({id:"phishing",t:"Spam and Phishing",c:"Technology",i:"🎣",w:"Phishing",r:["email","malware","cybersecurity","passwords","textmessaging"],
p:[["Unwanted messages","Spam is junk email and messages sent in bulk."],["Fishing for secrets","Phishing tricks people into revealing passwords or money."],["Stay alert","Checking links and senders helps avoid scams."]],
l:"Spam is unsolicited messages sent in bulk, usually by email, text message or social media, often advertising products or scams. Phishing is a type of online fraud in which criminals pretend to be trusted organizations, such as banks, delivery companies or employers, to trick people into revealing passwords, credit card numbers or other personal information, or into sending money or installing malware. The first known spam email was sent on 3 May 1978 by Gary Thuerk, a marketer at Digital Equipment Corporation, to about 400 users of ARPANET, the early internet. The word ‘spam’ comes from a 1970 Monty Python comedy sketch in which a group of Vikings sings ‘Spam’ over and over, drowning out conversation. The term ‘phishing’ appeared in the mid-1990s among hackers targeting America Online users; it is spelled with ‘ph’ like ‘phreaking’, an earlier term for phone hacking. Spam filters now block most junk email automatically, but phishing remains one of the most common ways cyberattacks begin. Variants include spear phishing aimed at specific people, ‘smishing’ by text message and ‘vishing’ by phone, and artificial intelligence now helps criminals write convincing messages.",
s:[["Warning signs","Phishing messages often create urgency, such as threats that an account will be closed, use slightly misspelled web addresses, ask for passwords or payment, or contain unexpected attachments. Legitimate banks do not ask customers to share passwords or one-time codes."],["Spam filters","Email services use spam filters that check messages against blocklists, look for suspicious words and patterns and use machine learning trained on billions of messages. Senders can also prove their identity with authentication systems."],["Laws","Laws such as the US CAN-SPAM Act of 2003 and the European Union’s rules on electronic communications require commercial messages to identify senders and allow recipients to unsubscribe, though criminals ignore them."],["Business email scams","In business email compromise, criminals impersonate executives or suppliers to persuade employees to transfer money. Such scams cost companies billions of dollars each year, according to the US FBI."]],
k:[["First spam email","Gary Thuerk, 3 May 1978"],["Name ‘spam’","From a 1970 Monty Python sketch"],["‘Phishing’ coined","Mid-1990s"],["Text-message phishing","Smishing"],["Best defense","Check senders and links; use multi-factor authentication"]],
d:[["1970","Monty Python’s ‘Spam’ sketch"],["1978","First spam email sent on ARPANET"],["1994","Lawyers’ ‘Green Card’ ad spams Usenet groups"],["1995","Phishing attacks target AOL users"],["2003","US CAN-SPAM Act passed"]],
s2:[["The Green Card spam","In April 1994, two lawyers posted an advertisement for immigration services to thousands of Usenet discussion groups, one of the first large commercial spams, provoking outrage from users."],["Phishing tests","Many companies send fake phishing emails to their own staff to train them to spot real attacks, and report results to help employees improve."]],
sp:["Spam the food","Spam, a canned meat introduced by Hormel Foods in 1937, is especially popular in Hawaii, Guam, South Korea and the Philippines. The company does not object to the email meaning but asks that it be written in lowercase."],
g:[["Spam","Unwanted bulk messages"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing information"],["Smishing","Phishing by text message"],["Spear phishing","Phishing aimed at a specific person"]],
f:["The first spam email was sent in 1978.","‘Spam’ comes from a Monty Python sketch.","Banks never ask for your password by email."],
fun:"Junk email is called ‘spam’ because of a comedy sketch in which Vikings keep singing the word ‘Spam’.",
tl:"Ang spam ay mga hindi hinihinging mensaheng ipinadadala nang maramihan. Nililinlang ng phishing ang mga tao upang ibigay ang password o pera, at nakatutulong ang pagsuri sa mga link at nagpadala upang maiwasan ang scam."});

ADDART({id:"generators",t:"Electric Generators and Transformers",c:"Technology",i:"⚡",w:"Electric generator",r:["powergrid","magnet","circuits","hydropower","nuclear"],
p:[["Making electricity","Generators turn motion into electricity using magnets."],["Faraday’s discovery","Michael Faraday discovered how in 1831."],["Changing voltage","Transformers raise and lower voltage to send power far."]],
l:"An electric generator converts mechanical energy, such as the motion of a turbine, into electricity. It works by electromagnetic induction, discovered by English scientist Michael Faraday in 1831: when a wire moves through a magnetic field, or a magnetic field changes around a wire, an electric current flows. Faraday built a simple generator, a copper disk spinning between magnet poles, and French instrument maker Hippolyte Pixii built an early hand-cranked generator in 1832. Powerful dynamos, developed by Werner von Siemens and others in the 1860s, made electric lighting and industry practical. Thomas Edison’s Pearl Street Station in New York began supplying direct current electricity in 1882. Transformers, which use induction to raise or lower voltage, were developed in the 1880s and made alternating current (AC) better for long-distance transmission, a key reason AC, championed by Nikola Tesla and George Westinghouse, won the ‘war of the currents’. Today, most of the world’s electricity comes from generators spun by steam from coal, gas or nuclear power plants, by falling water, by wind or by engines; solar panels are a major exception, producing electricity directly from light.",
s:[["How a generator works","In a power plant generator, a spinning magnet (the rotor) turns inside coils of wire (the stator). The changing magnetic field induces alternating current in the coils. Large generators can produce more than a thousand megawatts, enough for a city."],["Transformers","A transformer has two coils of wire wrapped around an iron core. Alternating current in one coil creates a changing magnetic field that induces current in the other. A coil with more turns gives a higher voltage. High voltages reduce energy lost as heat in long power lines."],["The war of the currents","In the late 1880s, Edison promoted direct current, while Westinghouse and Tesla promoted alternating current. AC’s ability to change voltage with transformers allowed power to travel far, and it powered the 1893 Chicago World’s Fair and the Niagara Falls power plant in 1895–1896."],["Backup generators","Portable and backup generators run on gasoline or diesel engines to provide power during outages, at construction sites and in remote areas. They must be used outdoors because their exhaust contains deadly carbon monoxide."]],
k:[["Electromagnetic induction","Michael Faraday, 1831"],["Early generator","Hippolyte Pixii, 1832"],["Pearl Street Station","New York, 1882"],["Transformer development","1880s"],["Current used in grids","Alternating current (AC)"]],
d:[["1831","Faraday discovers electromagnetic induction"],["1832","Pixii builds an early generator"],["1866","Siemens develops a self-exciting dynamo"],["1882","Edison’s Pearl Street Station opens"],["1886","William Stanley demonstrates an AC system with transformers"],["1895","Niagara Falls hydroelectric generators begin operating"]],
s2:[["Bicycle dynamos","Small generators called dynamos, turned by a bicycle wheel, power bicycle lights. Hand-crank radios and flashlights use the same principle."],["Motors and generators","Electric motors and generators are basically the same machine working in reverse. Electric cars use this to recover energy: when braking, their motors act as generators to recharge the battery."]],
sp:["Faraday’s answer","According to a popular story, when a politician asked Michael Faraday what use electricity was, he replied that one day the government would be able to tax it."],
g:[["Generator","A machine that turns motion into electricity"],["Induction","Creating current with a changing magnetic field"],["Transformer","A device that changes voltage"],["Alternating current","Current that reverses direction many times a second"]],
f:["Faraday discovered induction in 1831.","Transformers change voltage.","Most electricity comes from spinning generators."],
fun:"Electric car motors become generators when braking, turning motion back into electricity.",
tl:"Ginagawang kuryente ng generator ang galaw gamit ang magnet. Natuklasan ito ni Michael Faraday noong 1831, at itinataas at ibinababa ng transformer ang boltahe upang maipadala ang kuryente sa malayo."});

ADDART({id:"powertools",t:"Power Tools",c:"Technology",i:"🔧",w:"Power tool",r:["motors","battery","constructionmachines","lithium","machinetools"],
p:[["Tools with motors","Power tools use motors instead of muscle power."],["The electric drill","The electric drill was invented in 1889."],["Cordless freedom","Lithium-ion batteries made cordless tools powerful."]],
l:"Power tools are tools driven by an electric motor, gasoline engine or compressed air rather than by hand. Common examples include drills, saws, sanders, grinders, routers, nail guns and chainsaws. Arthur James Arnot and William Blanch Brain patented an electric drill in Melbourne, Australia, in 1889, and in 1895 the German company Fein made a portable handheld electric drill. In 1917, Black & Decker in the United States patented a drill with a pistol grip and trigger switch, a design still used today. Circular saws, jigsaws and belt sanders followed, and after World War II, smaller and cheaper tools let homeowners take up do-it-yourself projects. Chainsaws, made portable in the 1920s and 1950s, transformed forestry. Black & Decker introduced cordless tools in 1961, and the company built a battery-powered drill for NASA’s Apollo astronauts to collect lunar soil samples. Since the 2000s, lithium-ion batteries have made cordless tools nearly as powerful as corded ones. Power tools save time and effort but can cause serious injuries, so safety glasses, hearing protection, guards and training are important.",
s:[["Drills","Drills spin bits to make holes and drive screws. Hammer drills add a pounding action for masonry, and impact drivers deliver strong twisting blows to drive long screws. Drill presses mount drills on stands for precise holes."],["Saws","Circular saws cut straight lines through wood with a spinning toothed disk. Jigsaws move a thin blade up and down to cut curves. Table saws and miter saws are used in workshops for accurate cuts."],["The surprising chainsaw","An early form of chainsaw, a hand-cranked chain with cutting teeth, was developed by Scottish doctors in the late 18th century to help with difficult childbirths and bone surgery. Motorized chainsaws for cutting trees came much later."],["Safety","Kickback, when a saw blade catches and throws the tool back toward the user, is a common cause of injuries. Modern tools have blade guards, brakes and switches that stop the tool when released, and some table saws stop instantly if the blade touches skin."]],
k:[["Electric drill patent","Melbourne, Australia, 1889"],["Pistol-grip drill","Black & Decker, 1917"],["Cordless tools","1961"],["Modern battery","Lithium-ion"],["Safety gear","Goggles, ear protection, gloves"]],
d:[["1889","Electric drill patented in Australia"],["1895","Fein’s portable electric drill"],["1917","Black & Decker’s pistol-grip drill"],["1929","Early gasoline-powered chainsaw by Andreas Stihl"],["1961","First cordless drill"],["1971","Apollo 15 astronauts use a battery-powered lunar drill"]],
s2:[["DIY","Affordable power tools helped spread the do-it-yourself movement in the 1950s, as homeowners built furniture and renovated houses themselves. Home improvement stores and television shows encouraged the trend."],["Battery platforms","Many tool brands use the same battery across dozens of tools, from drills to lawn mowers, so users can swap batteries between tools."]],
sp:["Moon drill","On Apollo 15 in 1971, astronaut David Scott struggled to use a battery-powered drill to extract deep core samples from the hard lunar soil, a task that proved much harder than expected."],
g:[["Power tool","A tool driven by a motor or engine"],["Cordless","Running on batteries"],["Kickback","A sudden backward jerk of a tool"],["Impact driver","A tool that drives screws with strong blows"]],
f:["The electric drill was patented in 1889.","Cordless tools appeared in 1961.","An early chainsaw was invented for surgery."],
fun:"An early form of the chainsaw was invented by doctors in the 18th century to help with childbirth and surgery.",
tl:"Gumagamit ng motor ang mga power tool sa halip na lakas ng kalamnan. Inimbento ang electric drill noong 1889, at pinalakas ng mga lithium-ion battery ang mga cordless tool."});

ADDART({id:"tires",t:"Tires",c:"Technology",i:"🛞",w:"Tire",r:["rubber","cars","bicycle","friction","microplastics"],
p:[["Rubber on the road","Tires cushion vehicles and grip the road."],["Full of air","John Boyd Dunlop made the pneumatic tire practical in 1888."],["Tread and safety","Tread patterns channel water away to prevent skidding."]],
l:"Tires are rings of rubber fitted around wheels to provide grip, absorb bumps and support a vehicle’s weight. Early wheels had iron rims or solid rubber. In 1839, American inventor Charles Goodyear discovered vulcanization, heating rubber with sulfur to make it strong and elastic, which made rubber tires possible. Scottish engineer Robert William Thomson patented an air-filled, or pneumatic, tire in 1845, but it did not catch on. In 1888, Scottish veterinarian John Boyd Dunlop, living in Belfast, reinvented the pneumatic tire to make his son’s tricycle more comfortable, and it soon spread to bicycles. The French Michelin brothers made easily repairable, detachable tires and promoted them for cars in the 1890s. Michelin introduced the steel-belted radial tire in 1946, which lasted longer and handled better, and tubeless tires became common in the 1950s. Modern tires are made from natural and synthetic rubber, carbon black, steel and fabric, with tread patterns that grip the road and channel away water. Billions of tires are made each year, and worn tires create waste and shed tiny rubber particles, a major source of microplastic pollution.",
s:[["Parts of a tire","A tire has a tread that touches the road, sidewalls, and a bead that seals against the wheel rim. Inside, layers of fabric and steel cords called plies give strength. Radial tires have cords running across the tire at right angles to the direction of travel."],["Tread and grip","Tread grooves push water out from under the tire; when water builds up faster than it can be removed, the tire can ‘aquaplane’ and lose contact with the road. Many countries set a legal minimum tread depth, often 1.6 millimeters."],["Tire pressure","Correct air pressure improves safety, fuel economy and tire life. Many modern cars warn drivers when pressure is low. Under-inflated tires flex more, heat up and wear unevenly."],["Recycling","Old tires are recycled into playground surfaces, rubber asphalt and fuel for cement kilns. Large tire dumps can catch fire and burn for months, releasing toxic smoke."]],
k:[["Vulcanization","Charles Goodyear, 1839"],["Practical pneumatic tire","John Boyd Dunlop, 1888"],["Radial tire","Michelin, 1946"],["Main materials","Natural and synthetic rubber, carbon black, steel"],["Environmental issue","Tire-wear microplastics"]],
d:[["1839","Goodyear discovers vulcanization"],["1845","Thomson patents a pneumatic tire"],["1888","Dunlop’s pneumatic bicycle tire"],["1891","Michelin patents a detachable tire"],["1946","Michelin radial tire"],["1954","Tubeless tires become standard on some US cars"]],
s2:[["Why tires are black","Natural rubber is pale, but tires are black because carbon black is added, which strengthens rubber and protects it from sunlight. It greatly increases how long tires last."],["The Michelin Guide","The Michelin brothers created the Michelin Guide in 1900 to encourage people to drive more, and wear out more tires. It became famous for awarding stars to restaurants."]],
sp:["Airless tires","Companies are developing airless tires with flexible spokes that cannot go flat. NASA’s Moon rovers used wire-mesh wheels, and future lunar vehicles will need tires that work without air."],
g:[["Pneumatic","Filled with air"],["Vulcanization","Hardening rubber with sulfur and heat"],["Tread","The patterned surface that touches the road"],["Aquaplaning","Losing grip on a layer of water"]],
f:["Dunlop made the pneumatic tire practical in 1888.","Carbon black makes tires black and tough.","Michelin introduced the radial tire in 1946."],
fun:"The famous Michelin restaurant guide was created by a tire company to get people driving, and wearing out tires.",
tl:"Pinalalambot ng gulong ang biyahe at kumakapit ito sa daan. Ginawang praktikal ni John Boyd Dunlop ang gulong na may hangin noong 1888, at inaalis ng tread ang tubig upang hindi madulas."});

ADDART({id:"unicode",t:"Unicode and Character Encoding",c:"Technology",i:"🔣",w:"Unicode",r:["binary","emoji","comp","typography","langevo"],
p:[["Letters as numbers","Computers store every letter and symbol as a number."],["One code for all languages","Unicode gives a number to characters from almost every writing system."],["Emoji included","Emoji are part of Unicode too."]],
l:"Character encoding is the system computers use to store letters, numbers and symbols as binary numbers. Early computers used many incompatible codes. ASCII, the American Standard Code for Information Interchange, published in 1963, defined 128 characters, enough for English letters, digits and punctuation, but not for most other languages. Different countries created their own codes, and text sent between computers often turned into gibberish, a problem the Japanese call ‘mojibake’. To solve this, engineers from Xerox and Apple, including Joe Becker, Lee Collins and Mark Davis, began the Unicode project in 1987, aiming to give every character in every writing system a unique number. The first version of the Unicode Standard was published in 1991. Unicode now covers modern and historic scripts from Latin, Arabic and Chinese to Egyptian hieroglyphs, plus mathematical symbols and emoji; version 18.0, released in September 2026, contains 172,808 characters. The encoding called UTF-8, designed by Ken Thompson and Rob Pike in 1992, stores Unicode text efficiently and is used by about 98 percent of websites. The nonprofit Unicode Consortium, based in California, maintains the standard and approves new characters, including emoji.",
s:[["How it works","Each character has a code point, written like U+0041 for the capital letter A or U+1F600 for the grinning face emoji. Fonts provide the images of characters, while Unicode says which character each number represents."],["UTF-8","UTF-8 uses one byte for ASCII characters and up to four bytes for others, so English text stays compact and old ASCII files remain valid. Thompson and Pike reportedly sketched the design on a diner placemat in New Jersey."],["Scripts and languages","Unicode includes scripts used by small communities and ancient scripts studied by historians. Adding a script helps keep languages alive online, because people can type, search and share text in them."],["Emoji","Emoji, which began on Japanese phones in the late 1990s, were added to Unicode in 2010. Anyone can propose a new emoji, and the Unicode Consortium approves a small number each year."]],
k:[["ASCII","1963, 128 characters"],["Unicode project began","1987"],["Unicode 1.0","1991"],["Characters in Unicode 18.0","172,808 (September 2026)"],["Most common encoding","UTF-8"]],
d:[["1963","ASCII published"],["1987","Unicode project begins"],["1991","Unicode 1.0 published"],["1992","UTF-8 designed"],["2010","Emoji added to Unicode"],["2026","Unicode 18.0 released"]],
s2:[["Han unification","Chinese, Japanese and Korean share many characters derived from Chinese writing. Unicode gives shared characters one code point, a decision called Han unification that was debated because some characters look slightly different in each country."],["Mojibake","Mojibake, Japanese for ‘character transformation’, describes text that appears as meaningless symbols because it was decoded with the wrong encoding, such as Ã© appearing instead of é."]],
sp:["Adopt a character","The Unicode Consortium lets people ‘adopt’ a character, such as a letter or emoji, with a donation that supports its work."],
g:[["Character encoding","A system for storing text as numbers"],["Code point","The number assigned to a character"],["ASCII","An early 128-character code"],["UTF-8","The most common way to store Unicode text"]],
f:["ASCII had 128 characters.","Unicode 18.0 has 172,808 characters.","UTF-8 is used by about 98% of websites."],
fun:"The design for UTF-8, now used on almost every website, was reportedly sketched on a placemat in a diner.",
tl:"Iniimbak ng computer ang bawat titik at simbolo bilang numero. Binibigyan ng Unicode ng numero ang mga karakter mula sa halos lahat ng sistema ng pagsulat, at kasama rin dito ang mga emoji."});

ADDART({id:"gpus",t:"Graphics Processing Units",c:"Technology",i:"🎮",w:"Graphics processing unit",r:["chip","videogames","ai","supercomputers","cgi"],
p:[["Chips for graphics","GPUs were designed to draw 3D graphics for games."],["Thousands of cores","They do many simple calculations at once."],["Powering AI","GPUs now train most artificial intelligence systems."]],
l:"A graphics processing unit (GPU) is a computer chip designed to perform many calculations at the same time, originally to draw images for screens. Drawing 3D graphics means calculating the color of millions of pixels many times a second, a task that can be split into many small, similar jobs. While a central processing unit (CPU) has a few powerful cores that handle tasks one after another, a GPU has thousands of simpler cores working in parallel. Graphics accelerator cards such as 3dfx’s Voodoo, released in 1996, transformed 3D video games, and in 1999 Nvidia marketed its GeForce 256 as the world’s first GPU. Researchers soon realized GPUs could speed up scientific calculations, and Nvidia’s CUDA software, released in 2007, made programming them easier. In 2012, a neural network called AlexNet, trained on two gaming GPUs, won an image-recognition contest by a wide margin, sparking the deep-learning revolution. GPUs now train and run most large artificial intelligence models and power supercomputers, and huge data centers filled with them have made Nvidia one of the world’s most valuable companies. Demand for advanced chips has also made GPUs a subject of trade restrictions between countries.",
s:[["CPU vs GPU","A CPU is like a few expert chefs who can cook anything, while a GPU is like thousands of cooks each making one simple dish at the same time. For tasks with many identical operations, such as graphics or neural networks, the GPU is far faster."],["Gaming","GPUs handle lighting, textures, shadows and effects in games. Ray tracing, which simulates the paths of light rays for realistic reflections, became possible in real time on consumer GPUs from 2018."],["AI and data centers","Training a large AI model can require thousands of GPUs running for weeks or months. Data-center GPUs have special memory and connections and cost tens of thousands of dollars each. Companies such as Nvidia, AMD and Intel make GPUs, and tech giants design their own AI chips."],["Crypto mining","In the 2010s and early 2020s, people used GPUs to mine cryptocurrencies, causing shortages and high prices for gamers. Changes to the Ethereum network in 2022 ended much GPU mining."]],
k:[["Purpose","Parallel calculations for graphics"],["Early 3D card","3dfx Voodoo, 1996"],["Marketed as first GPU","Nvidia GeForce 256, 1999"],["CUDA released","2007"],["AI breakthrough","AlexNet, 2012"]],
d:[["1996","3dfx Voodoo graphics card released"],["1999","Nvidia GeForce 256"],["2007","CUDA released"],["2012","AlexNet trained on GPUs"],["2018","Real-time ray tracing on consumer GPUs"],["2022","AI chatbots drive demand for data-center GPUs"]],
s2:[["Graphics cards","In desktop computers, GPUs usually come on separate graphics cards with their own memory and cooling fans. Laptops and phones often have GPUs built into the main processor chip."],["Export controls","Since 2022, the United States has restricted exports of advanced AI chips to China, citing national security, and China has worked to develop its own chips."]],
sp:["From games to science","GPUs designed for video games have been used to fold proteins, simulate weather, analyze telescope images and model the universe."],
g:[["GPU","Graphics processing unit"],["CPU","Central processing unit"],["Parallel processing","Doing many calculations at once"],["Core","A processing unit within a chip"]],
f:["GPUs were designed for 3D graphics.","AlexNet was trained on GPUs in 2012.","GPUs train most large AI models."],
fun:"The AI revolution was sparked partly by a neural network trained on two graphics cards designed for video games.",
tl:"Idinisenyo ang GPU upang gumuhit ng 3D graphics para sa mga laro. Libu-libong simpleng kalkulasyon ang sabay nitong ginagawa, at ito na ngayon ang nagsasanay sa karamihan ng artificial intelligence."});

ADDART({id:"microcontrollers",t:"Microcontrollers and Mini Computers",c:"Technology",i:"🔌",w:"Microcontroller",r:["chip","robot","smarthome","coding","comp"],
p:[["Tiny computers inside things","Microcontrollers are small computers built into devices."],["Everywhere","Cars, toys, washing machines and remote controls contain them."],["Arduino and Raspberry Pi","Cheap boards let students build their own gadgets."]],
l:"A microcontroller is a small computer on a single chip, containing a processor, memory and input and output connections, designed to control a specific device. Unlike a personal computer, it usually runs one program, often for years, reading sensors and switching motors, lights or displays. Microcontrollers are hidden inside almost every electronic device: microwave ovens, washing machines, remote controls, toys, thermostats, cameras, medical devices and cars, which may contain dozens or even more than a hundred. Tens of billions are made every year. Intel introduced the first commercial microprocessor, the 4004, in 1971, and Texas Instruments’ TMS1000, released in 1974, is often called the first microcontroller. In 2005, a team at the Interaction Design Institute in Ivrea, Italy, created Arduino, an inexpensive, open-source microcontroller board that made it easy for artists, students and hobbyists to build interactive projects. The Raspberry Pi, a credit card-sized computer launched in the United Kingdom on 29 February 2012 to encourage children to learn programming, became one of the best-selling computers ever. Microcontrollers are at the heart of the Internet of Things, robotics and wearable devices.",
s:[["Embedded systems","A device with a built-in computer dedicated to one job is called an embedded system. Its software, called firmware, is stored in the chip. Embedded systems must often be reliable, cheap and very low in power use, running for years on small batteries."],["Arduino","Arduino boards connect easily to sensors, lights and motors and are programmed with simple code on a computer. They are used in school projects, art installations, weather stations and homemade robots."],["Raspberry Pi","The Raspberry Pi runs a full operating system and can be used as a desktop computer, media center or robot brain. Tens of millions have been sold, and it is used in classrooms, factories and even on the International Space Station."],["micro:bit","In 2016, the BBC gave a pocket-sized programmable board called the micro:bit to about a million British schoolchildren. It has buttons, lights and sensors and is now used in schools in many countries."]],
k:[["First microprocessor","Intel 4004, 1971"],["Early microcontroller","Texas Instruments TMS1000, 1974"],["Arduino","2005, Italy"],["Raspberry Pi launched","29 February 2012"],["Made each year","Tens of billions"]],
d:[["1971","Intel 4004 microprocessor"],["1974","TMS1000 microcontroller"],["2005","Arduino created"],["2012","Raspberry Pi launched"],["2015","Raspberry Pi computers sent to the ISS for student experiments"],["2016","BBC micro:bit given to UK students"]],
s2:[["Named after a bar","Arduino is named after a bar in Ivrea where the founders met, which itself was named after Arduin, an Italian king of the 11th century."],["Astro Pi","In the Astro Pi project, students write programs that run on Raspberry Pi computers aboard the International Space Station, taking measurements and photographs."]],
sp:["Computers in cars","A modern car may contain more than a hundred microcontrollers controlling engines, brakes, airbags, windows, mirrors and entertainment systems, linked by internal networks."],
g:[["Microcontroller","A tiny computer on a chip that controls a device"],["Embedded system","A device with a built-in dedicated computer"],["Firmware","Software stored inside a device"],["Sensor","A device that detects changes such as light or heat"]],
f:["Microcontrollers are inside most electronic devices.","Arduino was created in 2005.","The Raspberry Pi launched in 2012."],
fun:"Students’ programs have run on Raspberry Pi computers aboard the International Space Station.",
tl:"Ang microcontroller ay maliit na computer na nakapaloob sa mga device. Nasa kotse, laruan, washing machine at remote control ang mga ito, at pinapayagan ng murang Arduino at Raspberry Pi ang mga mag-aaral na gumawa ng sariling gadget."});

ADDART({id:"airtrafficcontrol",t:"Air Traffic Control",c:"Technology",i:"🗼",w:"Air traffic control",r:["flight","airports","radar","gps","radio"],
p:[["Guiding planes","Air traffic controllers keep aircraft safely apart."],["Towers and radar","Controllers use radio, radar and computers."],["English in the sky","English is the international language of aviation."]],
l:"Air traffic control (ATC) is the service that guides aircraft on the ground and in the air to prevent collisions and keep traffic flowing smoothly. Controllers in airport towers direct planes taxiing, taking off and landing, while controllers at approach and en route centers track aircraft across regions using radar and computer screens, assigning altitudes, routes and speeds and keeping planes safely separated. The first air traffic control tower opened at Croydon Airport near London in 1920, and in 1929 Archie League became one of the first controllers in the United States, guiding planes at St. Louis with colored flags. Radio and, after World War II, radar transformed the job. A collision between two airliners over the Grand Canyon in 1956, which killed 128 people, led the United States to create the Federal Aviation Agency in 1958 with authority over all air traffic. The International Civil Aviation Organization sets global standards, and English is the international language used between pilots and controllers. Modern systems increasingly use satellite positioning, with aircraft broadcasting their GPS positions, and many countries face shortages of trained controllers as air travel grows.",
s:[["How it works","A flight passes from ground control to the tower, then to departure and en route controllers, and finally to approach and tower controllers at its destination. Each handoff is coordinated by radio. Aircraft must keep minimum distances, such as about 5 nautical miles horizontally or 1,000 feet vertically in many areas."],["Radar and transponders","Primary radar detects aircraft by bouncing radio waves off them, while secondary radar receives signals from transponders on aircraft that report identity and altitude. Newer ADS-B systems let aircraft broadcast their satellite-derived positions every second."],["Phraseology","Pilots and controllers use standard phrases to avoid misunderstandings, such as ‘roger’ for ‘message received’ and the phonetic alphabet, Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, for letters. ‘Mayday’, from the French m’aider (help me), signals an emergency."],["A stressful job","Controllers must stay focused for long periods and make quick decisions, so they work in shifts with regular breaks. In 1981, more than 11,000 US controllers who went on an illegal strike were fired by President Ronald Reagan."]],
k:[["First control tower","Croydon Airport, London, 1920"],["Early US controller","Archie League, St. Louis, 1929"],["US Federal Aviation Agency","1958"],["International standards","ICAO"],["Language of aviation","English"]],
d:[["1920","Croydon Airport tower opens"],["1929","Archie League guides planes with flags"],["1935","First US air route traffic control center opens"],["1956","Grand Canyon midair collision"],["1958","US Federal Aviation Agency created"],["1981","US air traffic controllers’ strike"]],
s2:[["The busiest skies","On a busy day, controllers worldwide handle well over 100,000 flights. Some of the busiest airspace is around London, New York, Atlanta, Dubai and Chinese cities."],["Remote towers","Some small airports now use remote towers, where controllers watch high-definition camera feeds from a center many kilometers away instead of sitting in a tower."]],
sp:["Flag signals","Archie League stood in a wheelbarrow-like booth at the end of the runway with a checkered flag meaning ‘go’ and a red flag meaning ‘hold’, guiding pilots without radios."],
g:[["Air traffic controller","A person who directs aircraft"],["Transponder","A device that reports an aircraft’s identity and altitude"],["Separation","The minimum safe distance between aircraft"],["Mayday","An international distress call"]],
f:["The first control tower opened in 1920.","English is the language of aviation.","‘Mayday’ comes from French for ‘help me’."],
fun:"The emergency call ‘Mayday’ comes from the French phrase m’aider, meaning ‘help me’.",
tl:"Pinananatiling magkakalayo nang ligtas ng mga air traffic controller ang mga eroplano. Gumagamit sila ng radyo, radar at computer, at Ingles ang pandaigdigang wika ng abyasyon."});
